window.lectureSlides = [{"hidden":false,"text":"Transactions\nhttps://gist.github.com/oldwestbury/01dd0dd8d7a148872cdd506cc9e4026a","title":"Transactions","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Goals for this pair of lectures\nTransactions are a programming abstraction that enables the DBMS to handle recovery and concurrency for users.\nApplication: Transactions are critical for users\nEven casual users of data processing systems!\nFundamentals: The basics of how TXNs work\nTransaction processing is part of the debate around new data processing systems\nGive you enough information to understand how TXNs work, and the main concerns with using them","title":"Goals for this pair of lectures","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Intro to Transactions & Logging","title":"Intro to Transactions & Logging","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Today’s Lecture\nTransactions\nProperties of Transactions: ACID\nLogging\nConcurrency, scheduling & anomalies\nLocking: 2PL, conflict serializability, deadlock detection\n4","title":"Today’s Lecture","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"1. Transactions\n5","title":"1. Transactions","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"What you will learn about in this section\nOur “model” of the DBMS / computer\nTransactions basics\nMotivation: Recovery & Durability\nMotivation: Concurrency [next lecture]\n6\nLecture 7  >  Section 1","title":"What you will learn about in this section","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"High-level: Disk vs. Main Memory\nDisk:\nSlow\nSequential access\n(although fast sequential reads)\nDurable\nWe will assume that once on disk, data is safe!\nCheap\n7\nPlatters\nSpindle\nDisk head\nArm movement\nArm assembly\nTracks\nSector\nCylinder","title":"High-level: Disk vs. Main Memory","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Intro to Transactions & Logging","title":"Intro to Transactions & Logging","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"1. Transactions\n9","title":"1. Transactions","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"High-level: Disk vs. Main Memory\nDisk:\nSlow\nSequential access\n(although fast sequential reads)\nDurable\nWe will assume that once on disk, data is safe!\nCheap\n10\nPlatters\nSpindle\nDisk head\nArm movement\nArm assembly\nTracks\nSector\nCylinder\nhttps://www.ted.com/talks/kanawat_senanan_how_do_hard_drives_work","title":"High-level: Disk vs. Main Memory","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Random Access Memory (RAM) or Main Memory:\nFast\nRandom access, byte addressable\n~10x faster for sequential access\n~100,000x faster for random access!\nVolatile\nData can be lost if e.g. crash occurs, power goes out, etc!\nExpensive\nFor $100, get 16GB of RAM vs. 2TB of disk!\n11\nHigh-level: Disk vs. Main Memory","title":"Random Access Memory (RAM) or ","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Our model: Three Types of Regions of Memory\nLocal:  In our model each process in a DBMS has its own local memory, where it stores values that only it “sees”\nGlobal:  Each process can read from / write to shared data in main memory\nDisk:  Global memory can read from / flush to disk\nLog: Assume on stable disk storage- spans both main memory and disk…\nLocal\nGlobal\nMain Memory (RAM)\nDisk\n“Flushing to disk” = writing to disk from main memory\n1\n2\n3\nLog is a sequence from main memory -> disk\n4","title":"Our model: Three Types of Regions of Memory","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Keep in mind the tradeoffs here as motivation for the mechanisms we introduce\nMain memory: fast but limited capacity, volatile\nVs. Disk: slow but large capacity, durable\n13\nHigh-level: Disk vs. Main Memory\nHow do we effectively utilize both ensuring certain critical guarantees?","title":"Keep in mind the tradeoffs here as motivation for the mechanisms we introduce","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Transactions\n14","title":"Transactions","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Transactions: Basic Definition\nA transaction (“TXN”) is a sequence of one or more operations (reads or writes) which reflects a single real-world transition.\nSTART TRANSACTION\nUPDATE Product\tSET Price = Price – 1.99\tWHERE pname = ‘Gizmo’\nCOMMIT\nIn the real world, a TXN either happened completely or not at all","title":"Transactions: Basic Definition","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Transactions: Basic Definition\nA transaction (“TXN”) is a sequence of one or more operations (reads or writes) which reflects a single real-world transition.\nSTART TRANSACTION\nUPDATE Product\tSET Price = Price – 1\tWHERE price > 10.00\nCOMMIT\nIn the real world, a TXN either happened completely or not at all","title":"Transactions: Basic Definition","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Transactions: Basic Definition\nA transaction (“TXN”) is a sequence of one or more operations (reads or writes) which reflects a single real-world transition.\nIn the real world, a TXN either happened completely or not at all\nExamples:\nTransfer money between accounts\nPurchase a group of products\nRegister for a class (either waitlist or allocated)","title":"Transactions: Basic Definition","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Transactions in SQL\nIn “ad-hoc” SQL:\nDefault: each statement = one transaction\nIn a program, multiple statements can be grouped together as a transaction:\n18\nSTART TRANSACTION\nUPDATE Bank SET amount = amount – 100\nWHERE name = ‘Bob’\nUPDATE Bank SET amount = amount + 100\nWHERE name = ‘Joe’\nCOMMIT","title":"Transactions in SQL","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Transactions in SQL\nIn “ad-hoc” SQL:\nDefault: each statement = one transaction\nIn a program, multiple statements can be grouped together as a transaction:\n19\nSTART TRANSACTION\nUPDATE Bank SET amount = amount – 100\nWHERE name = ‘Bob’;\nUPDATE Bank SET amount = amount + 100\nWHERE name = ‘Joe’;\nCOMMIT","title":"Transactions in SQL","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Model of Transaction\nNote: For this course, we assume that the DBMS only sees reads and writes to data\nUser may do much more\nIn real systems, databases do have more info...","title":"Model of Transaction ","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Motivation for Transactions\nGrouping user actions (reads & writes) into transactions helps with two goals:\nRecovery & Durability:  Keeping the DBMS data consistent  and durable in the face of crashes, aborts, system shutdowns, etc.\nConcurrency:  Achieving better performance by parallelizing TXNs without creating anomalies\nThis lecture!\nNext lecture","title":"Motivation for Transactions","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Motivation\n1. Recovery & Durability of user data is essential for reliable DBMS usage\nThe DBMS may experience crashes (e.g. power outages, etc.)\nIndividual TXNs may be aborted (e.g. by the user)\nIdea: Make sure that TXNs are either durably stored in full, or not at all; keep log to be able to “roll-back” TXNs","title":"Motivation","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Protection against crashes / aborts\n23\nClient 1:\nINSERT INTO SmallProduct(name, price)\t\tSELECT pname, price\t\tFROM Product\nWHERE price <= 0.99\nDELETE Product\t\tWHERE price <=0.99\nWhat goes wrong?\nCrash / abort!","title":"Protection against crashes / aborts","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Protection against crashes / aborts\n24\nClient 1:\nSTART TRANSACTION\nINSERT INTO SmallProduct(name, price)\t\t\tSELECT pname, price\t\t\tFROM Product\nWHERE price <= 0.99\nDELETE Product\t\t\tWHERE price <=0.99\nCOMMIT OR ROLLBACK\nNow we’d be fine!  We’ll see how / why this lecture","title":"Protection against crashes / aborts","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Motivation\n2. Concurrent execution of user programs is essential for good DBMS performance.\nDisk accesses may be frequent and slow- optimize for throughput (# of TXNs), trade for latency (time for any one TXN)\nUsers should still be able to execute TXNs as if in isolation and such that consistency is maintained\nIdea: Have the DBMS handle running several user TXNs concurrently, in order to keep CPUs humming…","title":"Motivation","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Multiple users: single statements\n26\nClient 1: UPDATE Product\t\tSET Price = Price – 1.99\t\tWHERE pname = ‘Gizmo’Client 2:\tUPDATE Product\nSET Price = Price*0.5\t\tWHERE pname=‘Gizmo’\nTwo managers attempt to discount products concurrently-What could go wrong?","title":"Multiple users: single statements","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Multiple users: single statements\n27\nClient 1: START TRANSACTION\nUPDATE Product\t\t\tSET Price = Price – 1.99\t\t\tWHERE pname = ‘Gizmo’\nCOMMITClient 2: START TRANSACTION\nUPDATE Product\t\t\tSET Price = Price*0.5\t\t\tWHERE pname=‘Gizmo’\nCOMMIT\nNow works like a charm- we’ll see how / why next lecture…","title":"Multiple users: single statements","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"2. Properties of Transactions\n28","title":"2. Properties of Transactions","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"2. Properties of Transactions\n29","title":"2. Properties of Transactions","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"What you will learn about in this section\nAtomicity\nConsistency\nIsolation\nDurability\n30","title":"What you will learn about in this section","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Transaction Properties: ACID\nAtomic\nState shows either all the effects of txn, or none of them\nConsistent\nTxn moves from a state where integrity holds, to another where integrity holds\nIsolated\nEffect of txns is the same as txns running one after another (ie looks like batch mode)\nDurable\nOnce a txn has committed, its effects remain in the database\n31\nACID continues to be a source of great debate!","title":"Transaction Properties: ACID","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Lock Granularity\nLock can be of different levels\nDatabase\nTable\nPage\nRange\nRecord/tuple\n32","title":"Lock Granularity","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"ACID: Atomicity\nTXN’s activities are atomic: all or nothing\nIntuitively: in the real world, a transaction is something that would either occur completely or not at all\nTwo possible outcomes for a TXN\nIt commits: all the changes are made\nIt aborts: no changes are made\n33","title":"A","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"ACID: Consistency\nThe tables must always satisfy user-specified integrity constraints\nExamples:\nAccount number is unique\nStock amount can’t be negative\nSum of debits and of credits is 0\nHow consistency is achieved:\nProgrammer makes sure a txn takes a consistent state to a consistent state\nSystem makes sure that the txn is atomic\n34","title":"A","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"ACID: Isolation\nA transaction executes concurrently with other transactions\nIsolation: the effect is as if each transaction executes in isolation of the others.\nE.g. Should not be able to observe changes from other transactions during the run\n35","title":"AC","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"ACID: Durability\nThe effect of a TXN must continue to exist (“persist”) after the TXN\nAnd after the whole program has terminated\nAnd even if there are power failures, crashes, etc.\nAnd etc…\nMeans: Write data to disk\n36","title":"ACI","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Write-ahead Logging (WAL) Commit Protocol\nData on Disk\nMain Memory\nLog on Disk\nLog\nT\nA=1\nB=5\nA=0\nT: R(A), W(A)\nA: 01\nThis time, let’s try committing after we’ve written log to disk but before we’ve written data to disk… this is WAL!\nIf we crash now, is T durable?\nOK, Commit!","title":"Write-ahead Logging (WAL) Commit Protocol","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Write-ahead Logging (WAL) Commit Protocol\nData on Disk\nMain Memory\nLog on Disk\nT\nA=0\nT: R(A), W(A)\nA: 01\nThis time, let’s try committing after we’ve written log to disk but before we’ve written data to disk… this is WAL!\nIf we crash now, is T durable?\nOK, Commit!\nUSE THE LOG!\nA=1","title":"Write-ahead Logging (WAL) Commit Protocol","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"ACID: Durability\nThe effect of a TXN must continue to exist (“persist”) after the TXN\nAnd after the whole program has terminated\nAnd even if there are power failures, crashes, etc.\nAnd etc…\nMeans: Write data to disk\n39\nChange on the horizon? Non-Volatile Ram (NVRam). Byte addressable.","title":"ACI","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Challenges for ACID properties\nIn spite of failures: Power failures, but not media failures\nUsers may abort the program: need to “rollback the changes”\nNeed to log what happened\nMany users executing concurrently\nCan be solved via locking (we’ll see this next lecture!)\nAnd all this with… Performance!!\nThis lecture\nNext lecture","title":"Challenges for ACID properties","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"A Note: ACID is contentious!\nMany debates over ACID, both historically and currently\nMany newer “NoSQL” DBMSs relax ACID\nIn turn, now “NewSQL” reintroduces ACID compliance to NoSQL-style DBMSs…\nACID is an extremely important & successful paradigm, but still debated!","title":"A Note: ACID is contentious!","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Goal for this lecture: Ensuring Atomicity & Durability\nAtomicity:\nTXNs should either happen completely or not at all\nIf abort / crash during TXN, no effects should be seen\n42\nACID\nTXN 1\nTXN 2\nNo changes persisted\nAll changes persisted\nWe’ll focus on how to accomplish atomicity (via logging)\nCrash / abort\nDurability:\nIf DBMS stops running, changes due to completed TXNs should all persist\nJust store on stable disk","title":"Goal for this lecture: Ensuring Atomicity & Durability","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"The Log\nIs a list of modifications\nLog is duplexed and archived on stable storage.\nCan force write entries to disk\nA page goes to disk.\nAll log activities handled transparently the DBMS.\nAssume we don’t lose it!","title":"The Log","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Basic Idea: (Physical) Logging\nRecord UNDO information for every update!\nSequential writes to log\nMinimal info (diff) written to log\nThe log consists of an ordered list of actions\nLog record contains:\n<XID, location, old data, new data>\nThis is sufficient to UNDO any transaction!","title":"Basic Idea: (Physical) Logging","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Why do we need logging for atomicity?\nCouldn’t we just write TXN to disk only once whole TXN complete?\nThen, if abort / crash and TXN not complete, it has no effect- atomicity!\nWith unlimited memory and time, this could work…\nHowever, we need to log partial results of TXNs because of:\nMemory constraints (enough space for full TXN??)\nTime constraints (what if one TXN takes very long?)\nWe need to write partial results to disk!\n…And so we need a log to be able to undo these partial results!","title":"Why do we need logging for atomicity?","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"3. Atomicity & Durability via Logging\n46","title":"3. Atomicity & Durability via Logging","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"What you will learn about in this section\nLogging: An animation of commit protocols\n47","title":"What you will learn about in this section","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"A Picture of Logging","title":"A Picture of Logging","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"A Picture of Logging","title":"A Picture of Logging","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"A picture of logging\nData on Disk\nMain Memory\nLog on Disk\nLog\nT\nA=0\nB=5\nA=0\nT: R(A), W(A)","title":"A picture of logging","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"A picture of logging\nData on Disk\nMain Memory\nLog on Disk\nLog\nT\nA=1\nB=5\nA=0\nT: R(A), W(A)\nA: 01","title":"A picture of logging","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"A picture of logging\nData on Disk\nMain Memory\nLog on Disk\nLog\nT\nA=1\nB=5\nA=0\nT: R(A), W(A)\nA: 01\nOperation recorded in log in main memory!","title":"A picture of logging","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"What is the correct way to write this all to disk?\nWe’ll look at the Write-Ahead Logging (WAL) protocol\nWe’ll see why it works by looking at other protocols which are incorrect!\n53\nRemember: Key idea is to ensure durability while maintaining our ability to “undo”!","title":"What is the correct way to write this all to disk?","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Write-Ahead Logging (WAL)TXN Commit Protocol","title":"Write-Ahead Logging (WAL)","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Transaction Commit Process\nFORCE Write commit record to log\nAll log records up to last update from this TX are FORCED\nCommit() returns\nTransaction is committed once commit log record is on stable storage","title":"Transaction Commit Process","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Incorrect Commit Protocol #1\nData on Disk\nMain Memory\nLog on Disk\nLog\nT\nA=1\nB=5\nA=0\nT: R(A), W(A)\nA: 01\nLet’s try committing before we’ve written either data or log to disk…\nIf we crash now, is T durable?\nOK, Commit!\nLost T’s update!","title":"Incorrect Commit Protocol #1","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Incorrect Commit Protocol #2\nData on Disk\nMain Memory\nLog on Disk\nLog\nT\nA=1\nB=5\nA=0\nT: R(A), W(A)\nA: 01\nLet’s try committing after we’ve written data but before we’ve written log to disk…\nIf we crash now, is T durable?  Yes!  Except…\nOK, Commit!\nHow do we know whether T was committed??","title":"Incorrect Commit Protocol #2","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Improved Commit Protocol (WAL)","title":"Improved Commit Protocol (WAL)","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Improved Commit Protocol (WAL)","title":"Improved Commit Protocol (WAL)","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Write-ahead Logging (WAL) Commit Protocol\nData on Disk\nMain Memory\nLog on Disk\nLog\nT\nA=1\nB=5\nA=0\nT: R(A), W(A)\nA: 01\nThis time, let’s try committing after we’ve written log to disk but before we’ve written data to disk… this is WAL!\nIf we crash now, is T durable?\nOK, Commit!","title":"Write-ahead Logging (WAL) Commit Protocol","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Write-ahead Logging (WAL) Commit Protocol\nData on Disk\nMain Memory\nLog on Disk\nT\nA=0\nT: R(A), W(A)\nA: 01\nThis time, let’s try committing after we’ve written log to disk but before we’ve written data to disk… this is WAL!\nIf we crash now, is T durable?\nOK, Commit!\nUSE THE LOG!\nA=1","title":"Write-ahead Logging (WAL) Commit Protocol","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Write-Ahead Logging (WAL)\nDB uses Write-Ahead Logging (WAL) Protocol:\nMust force log record for an update before the corresponding data page goes to storage\nMust write all log records for a TX before commit\nEach update is logged! Why not reads?\n Atomicity\n Durability","title":"Write-Ahead Logging (WAL)","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Logging Summary\nIf DB says TX commits, TX effect remains after database crash\nDB can undo actions and help us with atomicity\nThis is only half the story…","title":"Logging Summary","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Concurrency & Locking","title":"Concurrency & Locking","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Concurrency & Locking","title":"Concurrency & Locking","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"4. Concurrency, Scheduling & Anomalies\n66","title":"4. Concurrency, Scheduling & Anomalies","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"What you will learn about in this section\nInterleaving & scheduling\nConflict & anomaly types\n67","title":"What you will learn about in this section","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Concurrency: Isolation & Consistency\nThe DBMS must handle concurrency such that…\nIsolation is maintained: Users must be able to execute each TXN as if they were the only user\nDBMS handles the details of interleaving various TXNs\nConsistency is maintained: TXNs must leave the DB in a consistent state\nDBMS handles the details of enforcing integrity constraints\nACID\nACID","title":"Concurrency: Isolation & Consistency","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Note the hard part…\n69\n…is the effect of interleaving transactions and crashes.\nSee 245 for the gory details!","title":"Note the hard part…","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Example- consider two TXNs:\nT1: START TRANSACTION\nUPDATE Accounts\tSET Amt = Amt + 100\nWHERE Name = ‘A’\nUPDATE Accounts\tSET Amt = Amt - 100\nWHERE Name = ‘B’\nCOMMIT\nT2: START TRANSACTION\nUPDATE Accounts\tSET Amt = Amt * 1.06\nCOMMIT\nT1 transfers $100 from B’s account to A’s account\nT2 credits both accounts with a 6% interest payment","title":"Example- consider two TXNs:","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Scheduling examples\n71\nT1\nT2\nA += 100\nB -= 100\nA *= 1.06\nB *= 1.06\nT1\nT2\nA += 100\nB -= 100\nA *= 1.06\nB *= 1.06\nA\nB\n$50\n$200\nA\nB\n$159\n$106\nA\nB\n$159\n$106\nStarting Balance\nSame result!\nSerial schedule T1,T2:\nInterleaved schedule A:","title":"Scheduling examples","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Scheduling examples\n72\nT1\nT2\nA += 100\nB -= 100\nA *= 1.06\nB *= 1.06\nT1\nT2\nA += 100\nB -= 100\nA *= 1.06\nB *= 1.06\nA\nB\n$50\n$200\nA\nB\n$159\n$106\nA\nB\n$159\n$112\nStarting Balance\nDifferent result than serial T1,T2!\nSerial schedule T1,T2:\nInterleaved schedule B:","title":"Scheduling examples","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":true,"text":"Scheduling examples\n73\nT1\nT2\nA += 100\nB -= 100\nA *= 1.06\nB *= 1.06\nT1\nT2\nA += 100\nB -= 100\nA *= 1.06\nB *= 1.06\nA\nB\n$50\n$200\nA\nB\n$153\n$112\nA\nB\n$159\n$112\nStarting Balance\nDifferent result than serial T2,T1 ALSO!\nSerial schedule T2,T1:\nInterleaved schedule B:","title":"Scheduling examples","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Conflict Types\nThus, there are three types of conflicts:\nRead-Write conflicts (RW)\nWrite-Read conflicts (WR)\nWrite-Write conflicts (WW)\nNotice no “RR Conflict”\nTwo actions conflict if they are part of different TXNs, involve the same variable, and at least one of them is a write\nInterleaving anomalies occur with / because of these conflicts between TXNs (but these conflicts can occur without causing anomalies!)","title":"Conflict Types","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Occurring with / because of a RW conflict\nClassic Anomalies with Interleaved Execution\n“Unrepeatable read”:\nT1\nT2\nR(A)\nR(A)\nT1 reads some data from A\nT2 writes to A\nThen, T1 reads from A again and now gets a different / inconsistent value\nR(A)\nW(A)\nC\nExample:","title":"Occurring ","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Occurring with / because of a WR conflict\nClassic Anomalies with Interleaved Execution\n“Dirty read” / Reading uncommitted data:\nT1\nT2\nW(A)\nA\nT1 writes some data to A\nT2 reads from A, then writes back to A & commits\nT1 then aborts- now T2’s result is based on an obsolete / inconsistent value\nR(A)\nW(A)\nC\nExample:","title":"Occurring with / because of ","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Classic Anomalies with Interleaved Execution\n“Inconsistent read” / Reading partial commits:\nT1\nT2\nW(A)\nT1 writes some data to A\nT2 reads from A and B, and then writes some value which depends on A & B\nT1 then writes to B- now T2’s result is based on an incomplete commit\nExample:\nW(B)\nC\nR(A)\nC\nR(B)\nW(C=A*B)\nAgain, occurring because of a WR conflict","title":"Classic Anomalies with Interleaved Execution","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Classic Anomalies with Interleaved Execution\nPartially-lost update:\nT1\nT2\nW(A)\nT1 blind writes some data to A\nT2 blind writes to A and B\nT1 then blind writes to B; now we have T2’s value for B and T1’s value for A- not equivalent to any serial schedule!\nExample:\nW(B)\nC\nW(A)\nC\nW(B)\nOccurring because of a WW conflict","title":"Classic Anomalies with Interleaved Execution","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Conflicts\nTwo actions conflict if they are part of different TXNs, involve the same variable, and at least one of them is a write\nT1\nT2\nR(A)\nR(B)\nW(A)\nW(B)\nR(A)\nR(B)\nW(A)\nW(B)\nW-R Conflict\nW-W Conflict","title":"Conflicts","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Conflicts\nTwo actions conflict if they are part of different TXNs, involve the same variable, and at least one of them is a write\nT1\nT2\nR(A)\nR(B)\nW(A)\nW(B)\nR(A)\nR(B)\nW(A)\nW(B)\nAll “conflicts”!","title":"Conflicts","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"The Conflict Graph\nLet’s now consider looking at conflicts at the TXN level\nConsider a graph where the nodes are TXNs, and there is an edge from Ti Tj if any actions in Ti precede and conflict with any actions in Tj\nT1\nT2\nT1\nT2\nR(A)\nR(B)\nW(A)\nW(B)\nR(A)\nR(B)\nW(A)\nW(B)","title":"The Conflict Graph","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"“Good” vs. “bad” schedules\n82\nSerial Schedule:\nT1\nT2\nR(A)\nR(B)\nW(A)\nW(B)\nR(A)\nR(B)\nW(A)\nW(B)\nT1\nT2\nR(A)\nR(B)\nW(A)\nW(B)\nR(A)\nR(B)\nW(A)\nW(B)\nT1\nT2\nR(A)\nR(B)\nW(A)\nW(B)\nR(A)\nR(B)\nW(A)\nW(B)\nX\nInterleaved Schedules:\nNote that in the “bad” schedule, the order of conflicting actions is different than the above (or any) serial schedule!","title":" “Good” vs. “bad” schedules","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"83\nSerial Schedule:\nT1\nT2\nR(A)\nR(B)\nW(A)\nW(B)\nR(A)\nR(B)\nW(A)\nW(B)\nT1\nT2\nR(A)\nR(B)\nW(A)\nW(B)\nR(A)\nR(B)\nW(A)\nW(B)\nT1\nT2\nR(A)\nR(B)\nW(A)\nW(B)\nR(A)\nR(B)\nW(A)\nW(B)\nX\nInterleaved Schedules:\nWhat can we say about “good” vs. “bad” conflict graphs?\nA bit complicated…","title":"25","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Example- consider two TXNs:\nT1 transfers $100 from B’s account to A’s account\nT2 credits both accounts with a 6% interest payment\nT1\nT2\nA += 100\nB -= 100\nA *= 1.06\nB *= 1.06\nTime\nWe can look at the TXNs in a timeline view- serial execution:","title":"Example- consider two TXNs:","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Example- consider two TXNs:\nT1 transfers $100 from B’s account to A’s account\nT2 credits both accounts with a 6% interest payment\nT1\nT2\nA += 100\nB -= 100\nA *= 1.06\nB *= 1.06\nTime\nThe TXNs could occur in either order… DBMS allows!","title":"Example- consider two TXNs:","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Example- consider two TXNs:\nT1\nT2\nA += 100\nB -= 100\nA *= 1.06\nB *= 1.06\nTime\nThe DBMS can also interleave the TXNs\nT2 credits A’s account with 6% interest payment, then T1 transfers $100 to A’s account…\nT2 credits B’s account with a 6% interest payment, then T1 transfers $100 from B’s account…","title":"Example- consider two TXNs:","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Example- consider two TXNs:\nWhat goes wrong here??\nT1\nT2\nA += 100\nB -= 100\nA *= 1.06\nB *= 1.06\nTime\nThe DBMS can also interleave the TXNs","title":"Example- consider two TXNs:","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":true,"text":"Recall: Three Types of Regions of Memory\nLocal:  In our model each process in a DBMS has its own local memory, where it stores values that only it “sees”\nGlobal:  Each process can read from / write to shared data in main memory\nDisk:  Global memory can read from / flush to disk\nLog: Assume on stable disk storage- spans both main memory and disk…\nLocal\nGlobal\nMain Memory (RAM)\nDisk\n“Flushing to disk” = writing to disk.\n1\n2\n3\nLog is a sequence from main memory -> disk\n4","title":"Recall: Three Types of Regions of Memory","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Why Interleave TXNs?\nInterleaving TXNs might lead to anomalous outcomes… why do it?\nSeveral important reasons:\nIndividual TXNs might be slow- don’t want to block other users during!\nDisk access may be slow- let some TXNs use CPUs while others accessing disk!\n89\nAll concern large differences in performance","title":"Why Interleave TXNs?","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Interleaving & Isolation\nThe DBMS has freedom to interleave TXNs\nHowever, it must pick an interleaving or schedule such that isolation and consistency are maintained\nMust be as if the TXNs had executed serially!\n90\nDBMS must pick a schedule which maintains isolation & consistency\n“With great power comes great responsibility”\nACID","title":"Interleaving & Isolation","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Scheduling examples\n91\nT1\nT2\nA += 100\nB -= 100\nA *= 1.06\nB *= 1.06\nT1\nT2\nA += 100\nB -= 100\nA *= 1.06\nB *= 1.06\nA\nB\n$50\n$200\nA\nB\n$159\n$106\nA\nB\n$159\n$106\nStarting Balance\nSame result!\nSerial schedule T1,T2:\nInterleaved schedule A:","title":"Scheduling examples","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Scheduling examples\n92\nT1\nT2\nA += 100\nB -= 100\nA *= 1.06\nB *= 1.06\nT1\nT2\nA += 100\nB -= 100\nA *= 1.06\nB *= 1.06\nA\nB\n$50\n$200\nA\nB\n$159\n$106\nA\nB\n$159\n$112\nStarting Balance\nDifferent result than serial T1,T2!\nSerial schedule T1,T2:\nInterleaved schedule B:","title":"Scheduling examples","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Scheduling examples\n93\nT1\nT2\nA += 100\nB -= 100\nA *= 1.06\nB *= 1.06\nT1\nT2\nA += 100\nB -= 100\nA *= 1.06\nB *= 1.06\nA\nB\n$50\n$200\nA\nB\n$153\n$112\nA\nB\n$159\n$112\nStarting Balance\nDifferent result than serial T2,T1 ALSO!\nSerial schedule T2,T1:\nInterleaved schedule B:","title":"Scheduling examples","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Scheduling examples\n94\nT1\nT2\nA += 100\nB -= 100\nA *= 1.06\nB *= 1.06\nThis schedule is different than any serial order!  We say that it is not serializable\nInterleaved schedule B:","title":"Scheduling examples","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Scheduling Definitions\nA serial schedule is one that does not interleave the actions of different transactions\nA and B are equivalent schedules if, for any database state, the effect on DB of executing A is identical to the effect of executing B\nA serializable schedule is a schedule that is equivalent to some serial execution of the transactions.\nThe word “some” makes this definition powerful & tricky!","title":"Scheduling Definitions","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":true,"text":"Serializable?\n96\nT1\nT2\nA += 100\nB -= 100\nA *= 1.06\nB *= 1.06\nSame as a serial schedule for all possible values of A, B = serializable\nSerial schedules:\nA\nB\nT1,T2\n1.06*(A+100)\n1.06*(B-100)\nT2,T1\n1.06*A + 100\n1.06*B - 100\nA\nB\n1.06*(A+100)\n1.06*(B-100)","title":"Serializable?","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":true,"text":"Serializable?\n97\nT1\nT2\nA += 100\nB -= 100\nA *= 1.06\nB *= 1.06\nNot equivalent to any serializable schedule = not serializable\nSerial schedules:\nA\nB\nT1,T2\n1.06*(A+100)\n1.06*(B-100)\nT2,T1\n1.06*A + 100\n1.06*B - 100\nA\nB\n1.06*(A+100)\n1.06*B - 100","title":"Serializable?","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"What else can go wrong with interleaving?\nVarious anomalies which break isolation / serializability\nOften referred to by name…\nOccur because of / with certain “conflicts” between interleaved TXNs\n98","title":"What else can go wrong with interleaving?","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":true,"text":"The DBMS’s view of the schedule\n99\nT1\nT2\nA += 100\nB -= 100\nA *= 1.06\nB *= 1.06\nT1\nT2\nR(A)\nR(A)\nW(A)\nW(A)\nR(B)\nW(B)\nR(B)\nW(B)\nEach action in the TXNs reads a value from global memory and then writes one back to it\nScheduling order matters!","title":"The DBMS’s view of the schedule","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Conflict Types\nThus, there are three types of conflicts:\nRead-Write conflicts (RW)\nWrite-Read conflicts (WR)\nWrite-Write conflicts (WW)\nWhy no “RR Conflict”?\nTwo actions conflict if they are part of different TXNs, involve the same variable, and at least one of them is a write\nInterleaving anomalies occur with / because of these conflicts between TXNs (but these conflicts can occur without causing anomalies!)\nSee next section for more!","title":"Conflict Types","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Occurring with / because of a RW conflict\nClassic Anomalies with Interleaved Execution\n“Unrepeatable read”:\nT1\nT2\nR(A)\nR(A)\nT1 reads some data from A\nT2 writes to A\nThen, T1 reads from A again and now gets a different / inconsistent value\nR(A)\nW(A)\nC\nExample:","title":"Occurring ","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Occurring with / because of a WR conflict\nClassic Anomalies with Interleaved Execution\n“Dirty read” / Reading uncommitted data:\nT1\nT2\nW(A)\nA\nT1 writes some data to A\nT2 reads from A, then writes back to A & commits\nT1 then aborts- now T2’s result is based on an obsolete / inconsistent value\nR(A)\nW(A)\nC\nExample:","title":"Occurring with / because of ","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Classic Anomalies with Interleaved Execution\n“Inconsistent read” / Reading partial commits:\nT1\nT2\nW(A)\nT1 writes some data to A\nT2 reads from A and B, and then writes some value which depends on A & B\nT1 then writes to B- now T2’s result is based on an incomplete commit\nExample:\nW(B)\nC\nR(A)\nC\nR(B)\nW(C=A*B)\nAgain, occurring because of a WR conflict","title":"Classic Anomalies with Interleaved Execution","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Classic Anomalies with Interleaved Execution\nPartially-lost update:\nT1\nT2\nW(A)\nT1 blind writes some data to A\nT2 blind writes to A and B\nT1 then blind writes to B; now we have T2’s value for B and T1’s value for A- not equivalent to any serial schedule!\nExample:\nW(B)\nC\nW(A)\nC\nW(B)\nOccurring because of a WW conflict","title":"Classic Anomalies with Interleaved Execution","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"5. Conflict Serializability, Locking & Deadlock\n105","title":"5. Conflict Serializability, Locking & Deadlock","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"What you will learn about in this section\nRECAP: Concurrency\nConflict Serializability\nDAGs & Topological Orderings\nStrict 2PL\nDeadlocks\n106","title":"What you will learn about in this section","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Recall: Concurrency as Interleaving TXNs\nFor our purposes, having TXNs occur concurrently means interleaving their component actions (R/W)\n107\nWe call the particular order of interleaving a schedule\nT1\nT2\nR(A)\nR(B)\nW(A)\nW(B)\nSerial Schedule:\nR(A)\nR(B)\nW(A)\nW(B)\nT1\nT2\nR(A)\nR(B)\nW(A)\nW(B)\nInterleaved Schedule:\nR(A)\nR(B)\nW(A)\nW(B)","title":"Recall: Concurrency as Interleaving TXNs","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Recall: “Good” vs. “bad” schedules\n108\nWe want to develop ways of discerning “good” vs. “bad” schedules\nSerial Schedule:\nT1\nT2\nR(A)\nR(B)\nW(A)\nW(B)\nR(A)\nR(B)\nW(A)\nW(B)\nT1\nT2\nR(A)\nR(B)\nW(A)\nW(B)\nR(A)\nR(B)\nW(A)\nW(B)\nT1\nT2\nR(A)\nR(B)\nW(A)\nW(B)\nR(A)\nR(B)\nW(A)\nW(B)\nX\nInterleaved Schedules:\nWhy?","title":"Recall: “Good” vs. “bad” schedules","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Ways of Defining “Good” vs. “Bad” Schedules\nRecall from last time: we call a schedule serializable if it is equivalent to some serial schedule\nWe used this as a notion of a “good” interleaved schedule, since a serializable schedule will maintain isolation & consistency\nNow, we’ll define a stricter, but very useful variant:\nConflict serializability\nWe’ll need to define conflicts first..","title":"Ways of Defining “Good” vs. “Bad” Schedules","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"T1\nT2\nR(A)\nR(B)\nW(A)\nW(B)\nR(A)\nR(B)\nW(A)\nW(B)","title":"T","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Conflicts\nTwo actions conflict if they are part of different TXNs, involve the same variable, and at least one of them is a write\nT1\nT2\nR(A)\nR(B)\nW(A)\nW(B)\nR(A)\nR(B)\nW(A)\nW(B)\nW-R Conflict\nW-W Conflict","title":"Conflicts","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Conflicts\nTwo actions conflict if they are part of different TXNs, involve the same variable, and at least one of them is a write\nT1\nT2\nR(A)\nR(B)\nW(A)\nW(B)\nR(A)\nR(B)\nW(A)\nW(B)\nAll “conflicts”!","title":"Conflicts","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Conflict Serializability\nTwo schedules are conflict equivalent if:\nThey involve the same actions of the same TXNs\nEvery pair of conflicting actions of two TXNs are ordered in the same way\nSchedule S is conflict serializable if S is conflict equivalent to some serial schedule\nConflict serializable  serializable\nSo if we have conflict serializable, we have consistency & isolation!","title":"Conflict Serializability","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Recall: “Good” vs. “bad” schedules\n114\nConflict serializability also provides us with an operative notion of “good” vs. “bad” schedules!\nSerial Schedule:\nT1\nT2\nR(A)\nR(B)\nW(A)\nW(B)\nR(A)\nR(B)\nW(A)\nW(B)\nT1\nT2\nR(A)\nR(B)\nW(A)\nW(B)\nR(A)\nR(B)\nW(A)\nW(B)\nT1\nT2\nR(A)\nR(B)\nW(A)\nW(B)\nR(A)\nR(B)\nW(A)\nW(B)\nX\nInterleaved Schedules:\nNote that in the “bad” schedule, the order of conflicting actions is different than the above (or any) serial schedule!","title":"Recall: “Good” vs. “bad” schedules","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Note: Conflicts vs. Anomalies\nConflicts are things we talk about to help us characterize different schedules\nPresent in both “good” and “bad” schedules\nAnomalies are instances where isolation and/or consistency is broken because of a “bad” schedule\nWe often characterize different anomaly types by what types of conflicts predicated them","title":"Note: Conflicts vs. Anomalies","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"The Conflict Graph\nLet’s now consider looking at conflicts at the TXN level\nConsider a graph where the nodes are TXNs, and there is an edge from Ti Tj if any actions in Ti precede and conflict with any actions in Tj\nT1\nT2\nT1\nT2\nR(A)\nR(B)\nW(A)\nW(B)\nR(A)\nR(B)\nW(A)\nW(B)","title":"The Conflict Graph","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":true,"text":"T0\nT1\nR(A)\nR(B)\nW(A)\nW(B)\nR(A)\nR(B)\nT1\nT2\nR(A)\nR(B)\nW(A)\nW(B)\nR(A)\nR(B)\nW(A)\nW(B)","title":"T0","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"118\nSerial Schedule:\nT1\nT2\nR(A)\nR(B)\nW(A)\nW(B)\nR(A)\nR(B)\nW(A)\nW(B)\nT1\nT2\nR(A)\nR(B)\nW(A)\nW(B)\nR(A)\nR(B)\nW(A)\nW(B)\nT1\nT2\nR(A)\nR(B)\nW(A)\nW(B)\nR(A)\nR(B)\nW(A)\nW(B)\nX\nInterleaved Schedules:\nWhat can we say about “good” vs. “bad” conflict graphs?\nA bit complicated…","title":"85","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"T1\nT2\nR(A)\nR(B)\nW(A)\nW(B)\nR(A)\nR(B)\nW(A)\nW(B)","title":"T","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"120\nSerial Schedule:\nX\nInterleaved Schedules:\nWhat can we say about “good” vs. “bad” conflict graphs?\nT1\nT2\nT1\nT2\nT1\nT2\nTheorem: Schedule is conflict serializable if and only if its conflict graph is acyclic\nSimple!","title":"87","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Isolation Levels\nPresentation on the 4 isolation levels in MYSQL/MariaDB","title":"Isolation Levels","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Let’s unpack this notion of acyclic conflict graphs…","title":"Let’s unpack this notion of acyclic conflict graphs…","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"DAGs & Topological Orderings\nA topological ordering of a directed graph is a linear ordering of its vertices that respects all the directed edges\nA directed acyclic graph (DAG) always has one or more topological orderings\n(And there exists a topological ordering if and only if there are no directed cycles)","title":"DAGs & Topological Orderings","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"DAGs & Topological Orderings\nEx: What is one possible topological ordering here?\n1\n3\n2\n0\nEx: 0, 1, 2, 3  (or: 0, 1, 3, 2)","title":"DAGs & Topological Orderings","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"DAGs & Topological Orderings\nEx: What is one possible topological ordering here?\n1\n3\n2\n0\nThere is none!","title":"DAGs & Topological Orderings","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Connection to conflict serializability\nIn the conflict graph, a topological ordering of nodes corresponds to a serial ordering of TXNs\nThus an acyclic conflict graph  conflict serializable!\nTheorem: Schedule is conflict serializable if and only if its conflict graph is acyclic","title":"Connection to conflict ","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"T0\nr0(A)\nw0(A)\nro(B)\nw0(B)\nc0\nT1\nr1(A)\nr1(B)\nc1\nConsider the following two transactions and schedule (time goes from left to right). Is this\nschedule conflict-serializable? Explain why or why not.","title":"T0","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Strict Two-Phase Locking\nWe consider locking- specifically, strict two-phase locking- as a way to deal with concurrency, because is guarantees conflict serializability (if it completes- see upcoming…)\nAlso (conceptually) straightforward to implement, and transparent to the user!","title":"Strict Two-Phase Locking","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Strict Two-phase Locking (Strict 2PL) Protocol:\nTXNs obtain:\nAn X (exclusive) lock on object before writing.\nIf a TXN holds, no other TXN can get a lock (S or X) on that object.\nAn S (shared) lock on object before reading\nIf a TXN holds, no other TXN can get an X lock on that object\nAll locks held by a TXN are released when TXN completes.\nNote: Terminology here- “exclusive”, “shared”- meant to be intuitive- no tricks!","title":"Strict Two-phase Locking (Strict 2PL) Protocol:","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Picture of 2-Phase Locking (2PL)\nTime\nStrict 2PL\n0 locks\n# Locks the TXN has\nLock Acquisition\nLock Release\nOn TXN commit!","title":"Picture of 2-Phase Locking (2PL)","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Strict 2PL\nTheorem: Strict 2PL allows only schedules whose dependency graph is acyclic\nTherefore, Strict 2PL only allows conflict serializable  serializable schedules\nProof Intuition: In strict 2PL, if there is an edge Ti  Tj (i.e. Ti and Tj conflict) then Tj needs to wait until Ti is finished – so cannot have an edge Tj  Ti","title":"Strict 2PL","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Strict 2PL\nIf a schedule follows strict 2PL and locking, it is conflict serializable…\n…and thus serializable\n…and thus maintains isolation & consistency!\nNot all serializable schedules are allowed by strict 2PL.\nSo let’s use strict 2PL, what could go wrong?\nDeadlock","title":"Strict 2PL","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Lock Granularity\nLock can be of different levels\nDatabase\nTable\nPage\nRecord\n133","title":"Lock Granularity","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Transactions\nSolution for both concurrency and failures\nA transaction is a sequence of one or more SQL\noperations treated as a unit\nTransactions appear to run in isolation\nIf the system fails, each transaction’s changes are\nreflected either entirely or not at all\n134","title":"Transactions","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Serializability\nOperations may be\ninterleaved, but execution\nmust be equivalent to some\nsequential (serial) order\nof all transactions\n Overhead\n Reduction in concurrency\nData\n.  .  .\nDBMS\nData\n(ACID Properties)  Isolation\n135","title":"Serializability","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Weaker “Isolation Levels”\nRead Uncommitted\nRead Committed\nRepeatable Read\n Overhead    Concurrency\n Consistency Guarantees\nData\n.  .  .\nDBMS\nData\n(ACID Properties)  Isolation\n136","title":"Weaker “Isolation Levels”","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Isolation Levels\nPer transaction\n“In the eye of the beholder”\nMy transaction is\nRepeatable Read\nMy transaction is\nRead Uncommitted\nData\n.  .  .\nDBMS\nData\n137","title":"Isolation Levels","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Anomalies\nDirty Read\nNon-repeatable read\nPhantom Read\n138","title":"Anomalies","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Dirty Read\n139\nSource: https://vladmihalcea.com/dirty-read/","title":"Dirty Read","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Non Repeatable Read\n140\nSource: https://vladmihalcea.com/non-repeatable-read/","title":"Non Repeatable Read","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Phantom Read\n141\nSource: https://vladmihalcea.com/phantom-read/","title":"Phantom Read","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"id\nname\nage\n1\nAlice\n20\n2\nBob\n25\nusers\nExample\n142\nSQL data: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/TeachingOW/DBMS-SQL-Labs/refs/heads/main/inclass/users.sql","title":"id","width":1440,"height":855,"links":["https://raw.githubusercontent.com/TeachingOW/DBMS-SQL-Labs/refs/heads/main/inclass/users.sql"]},{"hidden":false,"text":"id\nname\nage\n1\nAlice\n20\n2\nBob\n25\nusers\nExample\n143","title":"id","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Read phenomena / Isolation Level\nTransaction 1\nTransaction 2\nBEGIN; SELECT age FROM users WHERE id = 1; -- retrieves 20\nBEGIN; UPDATE users SET age = 21 WHERE id = 1; -- no commit here\nSELECT age FROM users WHERE id = 1; -- READ UNCOMMITTED retrieves 21 (dirty read) -- READ COMMITTED retrieves 20 (dirty read has been avoided) -- REPEATABLE READ retrieves 20 (dirty read has been avoided) -- SERIALIZABLE retrieves 20 (dirty read has been avoided)\nCOMMIT;\nROLLBACK;\n144","title":" ","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Transaction 1\nTransaction 2\nBEGIN; SELECT age FROM users WHERE id = 1; -- retrieves 20\nBEGIN;\nUPDATE users SET age = 21 WHERE id = 1;\nCOMMIT;\nSELECT age FROM users WHERE id = 1; -- READ UNCOMMITTED retrieves 21 (non-repeatable read) -- READ COMMITTED retrieves 21 (non-repeatable read) -- REPEATABLE READ retrieves 20 (non-repeatable read has been avoided) -- SERIALIZABLE retrieves 20 (non-repeatable read has been avoided) COMMIT;\nRead phenomena / Isolation Level\n145","title":"Transaction 1","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Transaction 1\nTransaction 2\nBEGIN; SELECT name FROM users WHERE age > 17; -- retrieves Alice and Bob\nBEGIN; INSERT INTO users VALUES (3, 'Carol', 26); COMMIT;\nSELECT name FROM users WHERE age > 17; -- READ UNCOMMITTED retrieves Alice, Bob and Carol (phantom read) -- READ COMMITTED retrieves Alice, Bob and Carol (phantom read) -- REPEATABLE READ retrieves Alice, Bob and Carol (phantom read) -- SERIALIZABLE retrieves Alice and Bob (phantom read has been avoided) COMMIT;\nRead phenomena / Isolation Level\n146","title":"Transaction 1","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Set Session Transaction Isolation Level Serializable;\nStart Transaction  Read ONLY;\nSelect Avg(age) From Users;\nSelect Max(age) From Users;\nRead Only transactions\nHelps system optimize performance\nIndependent of isolation level\n147","title":"Set Session Transaction Isolation Level Serializable;","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"dirty reads\nnonrepeatable\nreads\nphantoms\nRead Uncommitted\nY\nY\nY\nRead Committed\nN\nY\nY\nRepeatable Read\nN\nN\nY*\nSerializable\nN\nN\nN\nIsolation Levels: Summary\nMySQL includes more optimizations*\n148","title":"dirty","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":true,"text":"Example I\nTransaction 1\nTransaction 2\nSET SESSION TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL Serializable;\nBEGIN;\nSET SESSION TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL Serializable;\nBEGIN;\nSELECT COUNT(*) FROM users;\nSELECT * FROM users;\nINSERT INTO `users` VALUES (4,'Dave', 26);\nINSERT INTO `users` VALUES (5,'Eric', 26);\n149","title":"Example I","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Deadlock Example\nTransaction 1\nTransaction 2\nSET SESSION TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL Serializable;\nBEGIN;\nSET SESSION TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL Serializable;\nBEGIN;\nSELECT COUNT(*) FROM users;\nSELECT * FROM users;\nINSERT INTO `users` VALUES (4,Dave’, 26);\nINSERT INTO `users` VALUES (5,’Eric’, 26);\n150","title":"Deadlock Example","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Set Transaction Read Only;\nSet Transaction Isolation Level Serlizable;\nSelect Avg(GPA) From Student;\nSelect Max(GPA) From Student;\nRead Only transactions\nHelps system optimize performance\nIndependent of isolation level\n151","title":"Set Transaction Read Only;","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"dirty reads\nnonrepeatable\nreads\nphantoms\nRead Uncommitted\nY\nY\nY\nRead Committed\nN\nY\nY\nRepeatable Read\nN\nN\nY*\nSerializable\nN\nN\nN\nIsolation Levels: Summary\nMysql uses some optimizations*\n152","title":"dirty","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Example\nTransaction 1\nTransaction 2\nSET SESSION TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL Serializable;\nBEGIN;\nSET SESSION TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL Serializable;\nBEGIN;\nSELECT COUNT(*) FROM users;\nSELECT * FROM users;\nINSERT INTO `users` VALUES (4,Dave’, 26);\nINSERT INTO `users` VALUES (5,’Eric’, 26);\n153","title":"Example","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Example II\n154\nTransaction 1\nTransaction 2\nSET SESSION TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL Serializable;\nBEGIN;\nSET SESSION TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL Serializable;\nBEGIN;\nINSERT INTO `users` VALUES (6,'Fred', 26);\nINSERT INTO `users` VALUES (7,'George', 27);\nCommit;\nCommit;","title":"Example II","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Example III\n155\nTransaction 1\nTransaction 2\nSET SESSION TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL Serializable;\nBEGIN;\nSET SESSION TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL Serializable;\nBEGIN;\nINSERT INTO `users` VALUES (8,’Hany’, 28);\nINSERT INTO `users` VALUES (8,’Henry’, 28);\nCommit; (Try with Rollback;)\nCommit;","title":"Example III","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Example IV\nTransaction 1\nTransaction 2\nSet Session Transaction Isolation Level SERIALIZABLE;\nSet Session Transaction Isolation Level SERIALIZABLE;\nBEGIN; SELECT name FROM users WHERE id= 1;\nBEGIN;\nINSERT INTO users VALUES (110, 'Eve', 26);\nCOMMIT;\nCOMMIT;\n156","title":"Example IV","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Example IV\nTransaction 1\nTransaction 2\nSet Transaction Isolation Level SERIALIZABLE;\nSet Transaction Isolation Level SERIALIZABLE;\nBEGIN; SELECT name FROM users WHERE id= 1;\nBEGIN; INSERT INTO users VALUES (10, ‘Eve', 26); COMMIT;\nCOMMIT;\n157","title":"Example IV","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":true,"text":"Example V\nTransaction 1\nTransaction 2\nSet Transaction Isolation Level Repeatable read, Read Only;\nSet Transaction Isolation Level SERIALIZABLE;\nBEGIN; SELECT count(*) FROM users;\nBEGIN; INSERT INTO users VALUES (10, ‘Eve', 26); COMMIT;\nSELECT count(*) FROM users;\nCOMMIT;\n158","title":"Example V","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":true,"text":"Example V\nTransaction 1\nTransaction 2\nSet session Transaction Isolation Level SERIALIZABLE, Read Only;\nSet Transaction Isolation Level SERIALIZABLE;\nBEGIN; SELECT count(*) FROM users;\nBEGIN; INSERT INTO users VALUES (10, ‘Eve', 26); COMMIT;\nSELECT max(age) FROM users;\nCOMMIT;\n159","title":"Example V","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"160","title":"26","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":true,"text":"Example V\nTransaction 1\nTransaction 2\nSet Transaction Isolation Level SERIALIZABLE, Read Only;\nSet Transaction Isolation Level SERIALIZABLE;\nBEGIN; SELECT count(*) FROM users;\nBEGIN; INSERT INTO users VALUES (10, ‘Eve', 26); COMMIT;\nSELECT max(age) FROM users;\nCOMMIT;\n161","title":"Example V","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Isolation Levels: Summary\nStandard default: Repeatable read (InnoDB) in Mysql\nWeaker isolation levels\nIncreased concurrency + decreased overhead =\nincreased performance\nWeaker consistency guarantees\nIsolation level per transaction and “eye of the beholder”\nEach transaction’s reads must conform to its isolation level\n162","title":"Isolation Levels: Summary","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Picture of 2-Phase Locking (2PL)\nTime\nStrict 2PL\n0 locks\n# Locks the TXN has\nLock Acquisition\nLock Release\nOn TXN commit!","title":"Picture of 2-Phase Locking (2PL)","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Strict Two-phase Locking (Strict 2PL) Protocol:\nTXNs obtain:\nAn X (exclusive) lock on object before writing.\nIf a TXN holds, no other TXN can get a lock (S or X) on that object.\nAn S (shared) lock on object before reading\nIf a TXN holds, no other TXN can get an X lock on that object\nAll locks held by a TXN are released (unlocked) when TXN completes.\nExclusive lock is not compatible with a shared lock from another transaction.\nNote: Terminology here- “exclusive”, “shared”- meant to be intuitive- no tricks!","title":"Strict Two-phase Locking (Strict 2PL) Protocol:","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Picture of 2-Phase Locking (2PL)\nTime\nStrict 2PL\n0 locks\n# Locks the TXN has\nLock Acquisition\nLock Release\nOn TXN commit!","title":"Picture of 2-Phase Locking (2PL)","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Strict 2PL\nTheorem: Strict 2PL allows only schedules whose dependency graph is acyclic\nTherefore, Strict 2PL only allows conflict serializable  serializable schedules\nProof Intuition: In strict 2PL, if there is an edge Ti  Tj (i.e. Ti and Tj conflict) then Tj needs to wait until Ti is finished – so cannot have an edge Tj  Ti","title":"Strict 2PL","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Strict 2PL\nIf a schedule follows strict 2PL and locking, it is conflict serializable…\n…and thus serializable\n…and thus maintains isolation & consistency!\nNot all serializable schedules are allowed by strict 2PL.\nSo let’s use strict 2PL, what could go wrong?","title":"Strict 2PL","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"T0\nr0(A)\nWo(A)\nw0(B)\nc0\nT1\nr1(B)\nW1(A)\nc1","title":"T0","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Deadlock Detection: Example\nFirst, T1 requests a shared lock on A to read from it\nT1\nT2\nS(A)\nR(A)\nWaits-for graph:\nT1\nT2","title":"Deadlock Detection: Example","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Deadlock Detection: Example\nNext, T2 requests a shared lock on B to read from it\nT1\nT2\nS(B)\nR(B)\nS(A)\nR(A)\nWaits-for graph:\nT1\nT2","title":"Deadlock Detection: Example","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Deadlock Detection: Example\nT2 then requests an exclusive lock on A to write to it- now T2 is waiting on T1…\nT1\nT2\nX(A)\nS(B)\nR(B)\nS(A)\nR(A)\nWaits-for graph:\nT1\nT2\nW(A)\nWaiting…","title":"Deadlock Detection: Example","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Deadlock Detection: Example\nFinally, T1 requests an exclusive lock on B to write to it- now T1 is waiting on T2… DEADLOCK!\nT1\nT2\nX(B)\nX(A)\nS(B)\nR(B)\nS(A)\nR(A)\nWaits-for graph:\nT1\nT2\nW(A)\nW(B)\nCycle = DEADLOCK\nWaiting…\nWaiting…","title":"Deadlock Detection: Example","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"ERROR:  deadlock detected\nDETAIL:  Process 321 waits for ExclusiveLock on tuple of relation 20 of database 12002; blocked by process 4924.\nProcess 404 waits for ShareLock on transaction 689; blocked by process 552.\nHINT:  See server log for query details.\nThe problem?Deadlock!??!\nT1\nT2","title":"ERROR:  deadlock detected","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Deadlocks\nDeadlock: Cycle of transactions waiting for locks to be released by each other.\nTwo ways of dealing with deadlocks:\nDeadlock prevention\nDeadlock detection","title":"Deadlocks","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Deadlock Detection\nCreate the waits-for graph:\nNodes are transactions\nThere is an edge from Ti  Tj if Ti is waiting for Tj to release a lock\nPeriodically check for (and break) cycles in the waits-for graph","title":"Deadlock Detection","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Summary\nConcurrency achieved by interleaving TXNs such that isolation & consistency are maintained\nWe formalized a notion of serializability that captured such a “good” interleaving schedule\nWe defined conflict serializability, which implies serializability\nLocking allows only conflict serializable schedules\nIf the schedule completes… (it may deadlock!)","title":"Summary","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Presentation on Isolation Levels\nIn class\n10 minutes\nWhy are they needed?\nCompare them","title":"Presentation on Isolation Levels","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":true,"text":"R(A)\nW(A)\nR(A)\nW(B)\nR(B)\nT0\nt1\nt2\nR(A)\nW(A)\nR(A)\nW(B)\nR(B)\nT0\nt1\nt2\nS(A)\nX(A)\nS(A)\nS(B)","title":"R(A)","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Transactions","title":"Transactions","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Transactions\nIsolation Levels\nSlides are based on Slides from Prof. Widom and Prof. Peter Bailis","title":"  Transactions","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Transactions made a promise to the programmer about complexities they didn't have to worry about\nPeter Alvaro\nUniversity of California, Santa Cruz\n181","title":"Transactions","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Transactions\nIsolation Levels","title":"  Transactions","width":1440,"height":855,"links":[]},{"hidden":false,"text":"Practice and hosted labs\nTransactions, isolation and recovery foundations\nTransactions, commit and rollback\nIsolation levels and concurrent sessions\nTransactional trigger exercise\nYour task: Use two database sessions to demonstrate an anomaly. Repeat with a stated isolation level; roll back a change and explain the result. Use the recovery readings for WAL and distributed commit.\nHosted on GitHub Pages. Internet access required.","title":"Practice and hosted labs","width":1440,"height":855,"links":["https://teachingow.github.io/DBMS-SQL-Labs/labs/transactions-triggers/transactions.html","https://teachingow.github.io/DBMS-SQL-Labs/labs/transactions-triggers/isolation-levels.html","https://teachingow.github.io/DBMS-SQL-Labs/labs/transactions-triggers/bank-triggers.html"]}];
